                    coach_default:          `Decision logged.`,
                    coach_r1_wms:           `<strong>That is the could-kill-us threat.</strong> The untested WMS single point of failure already halted three sites for 48 hours and cost £1.1M — and the next failed update does it again. The others are real, but this is the one that can end the company, not just dent the quarter.`,
                    coach_r1_cyber:         `<strong>Cyber is a genuine domain — but is it the existential one?</strong> A competitor was hit, yes, yet your own 48-hour outage came from an untested WMS dependency, not an attacker. Do not let the headline risk crowd out the single point of failure that has already bitten you.`,
                    coach_r1_people:        `<strong>People fragility is real and feeds the others — but it rarely ends the company alone.</strong> Thin management redundancy and fatigue are why the WMS failover was never tested. Treat it as a contributing cause, and name the threat that can actually halt operations.`,
                    coach_r1_margin:        `<strong>A 6% margin is a vulnerability, not an existential threat.</strong> It thins your shock absorption, but it is a costly-but-survivable condition, not the thing that halts three sites overnight. Which single point of failure can actually stop the company?`,
                    coach_r1_sharp:        `<strong>Sharp triage.</strong> You isolated the WMS single point of failure as existential AND ranked the register by likelihood × impact instead of calling everything red. That is exactly the discipline that lets £6M fund what matters rather than spreading thin across fourteen items.`,
                    coach_r1_allred:        `<strong>Fourteen red items and a budget that funds maybe five.</strong> Treating everything as critical means nothing gets properly resourced — and you will fund breadth over the could-kill-us gaps. Which two or three vulnerabilities are genuinely existential, and did you rank them first?`,
                    coach_r1_cheapest:      `<strong>Cheapest-first is not prioritisation.</strong> Quick wins feel productive but can leave the existential WMS hole unfunded while you tidy the inexpensive corners. Rank by likelihood × impact, not by price tag.`,
                    coach_r1_recent:        `<strong>Fighting the last incident is how brittle firms stay brittle.</strong> The WMS matters — but resilience means anticipating the shock you cannot yet name, not just patching the one that already happened. Diagnose across all six domains.`,
                    coach_r1_lowhonesty:    `<strong>You have skipped the root cause.</strong> The board named it: a culture that prizes efficiency over adaptability is what stripped the slack and skipped the failover test. Patch the systems without naming the culture and Calderwood re-creates the fragility within a year.`,
                    coach_r2_ebm:           `<strong>That is the evidence-based-management discipline.</strong> Rating each intervention on the quality and source of its evidence — scientific, organisational, practitioner and stakeholder — is exactly how you stop funding fads and start funding what works.`,
                    coach_r2_vendor:        `<strong>Vendor case studies are the weakest evidence there is.</strong> The supplier that sells the fix is not a neutral source, and a single pilot is not generalisable evidence. This is precisely how 'risk management on luck' gets funded.`,
                    coach_r2_consensus:     `<strong>Strength of feeling is not evidence.</strong> Funding what leadership feels most strongly about is how ideology and seniority crowd out the data. Appraise the evidence, not the volume of the advocate.`,
                    coach_r2_benchmark:     `<strong>Copying peers is not appraisal.</strong> What the sector is buying this year may itself be a fad — and your context differs. Evidence-based management means judging the evidence for your organisation, not following the herd.`,
                    coach_r2_aireject:      `<strong>Good — you challenged the fashionable fix.</strong> A single vendor case study is weak, anecdotal evidence; £2.4M on it would crowd out the well-evidenced WMS failover and blow your cap. That is the discipline the board has been missing.`,
                    coach_r2_aipilot:       `<strong>A small evidence-gathering pilot is a defensible middle path.</strong> You avoid a £2.4M bet on weak evidence while keeping the option open — but it still spends £0.4M of a hard £6M cap, so guard the rest of the portfolio.`,
                    coach_r2_aifund:        `<strong>You have just spent £2.4M on a single case study.</strong> That is the fad the board warned about — and it eats 40% of a £6M cap that the £14M wish-list was already straining. Now the well-evidenced WMS failover and the adaptive capacity have to fit in what is left.`,
                    coach_r2_failmoderate:  `<strong>The WMS failover is stronger evidence than 'moderate'.</strong> Continuity engineering against a known single point of failure is robust, generalisable practice — and this one already cost you £1.1M. Treating it as 'no rush' risks leaving the existential gap underfunded.`,
                    coach_r2_failweak:      `<strong>That is the all-progression trap forming.</strong> Dismissing the tested failover as 'just the last incident' is how the could-kill-us hole stays open. The evidence for continuity engineering on a proven single point of failure is strong, not weak.`,
                    coach_r3_balanced:      `<strong>A genuine balance.</strong> You funded defensive robustness and progressive adaptive capacity rather than over-weighting either, with a real cultural slice. Robustness protects against yesterday's shock; adaptive capacity prepares for the one you cannot yet name. Holding both is the heart of resilience.`,
                    coach_r3_alldefence:    `<strong>You have made Calderwood robust against the last disruption — and nothing else.</strong> Every system now has a backup, but the next shock will be a demand collapse or a new client model, not the WMS again. Where is the progressive resilience — the capacity to sense and adapt to what you cannot yet name?`,
                    coach_r3_allprogress:   `<strong>Admirable adaptive capacity — built on an unpatched hole.</strong> Sensing and learning loops are progressive resilience, but if the defensive spend is this thin the existential single points of failure stay exposed. Adaptability does not excuse leaving the could-kill-us gaps open.`,
                    coach_r3_noculture:     `<strong>You have patched the systems and skipped the culture.</strong> The root cause the board named is a culture that prizes efficiency over adaptability — the same culture that stripped the slack and skipped the failover test. Fund no culture change and the fragility quietly returns.`,
                    coach_r3_wmsopen:       `<strong>The existential gap is still open.</strong> You funded sensing, learning and culture — admirable — but the untested WMS dependency that just cost £1.1M still has no fallback. The next failed update halts three sites again. The could-kill-us vulnerability has to be funded first.`,
                    coach_r4_evidence:      `<strong>That is the honest, board-ready answer.</strong> An evidence-rated portfolio that balances defence with adaptive capacity genuinely is different from a generic risk register — and you can show the board exactly why each pound was spent.`,
                    coach_r4_more:          `<strong>"More of the same" is the answer the board feared.</strong> A bigger budget on the same approach is precisely the risk management on luck they want to end. If you cannot say how this differs, you have not changed anything but the number.`,
                    coach_r4_insurance:     `<strong>Insurance and backups are defensive only.</strong> They transfer and absorb known shocks but buy zero adaptive capacity and change no culture. 'We are covered' is exactly the brittleness that produced the outage — robustness is necessary, not sufficient.`,
                    coach_r4_project:       `<strong>Resilience is not a project you complete.</strong> Deliver it in 18 months and close it out, and when cost pressure returns next year the slack gets stripped back out and Calderwood becomes brittle again. It is a capability you sustain and a culture that keeps prizing adaptability.`,
                    coach_r4_audit:         `<strong>An annual audit is better than nothing, but it is still reactive.</strong> Re-chartering a programme after the next break is how brittle firms operate. Build the capability to sense and adapt continuously, not a cycle of break-and-fix.`,
                    coach_r4_recovery:      `<strong>Recovery time alone is a lagging indicator.</strong> By the time it moves, the next incident has already happened. Pair it with leading indicators of the resilience capability so you see fragility building before it bites.`,
                    coach_r4_spend:         `<strong>Reporting budget spent measures inputs, not resilience.</strong> Committing £6M on schedule tells the board nothing about whether the organisation can actually withstand the next shock. Measure the outcome — recovery time and the resilience capability — not the cheque.`,
                    coach_r4_strong:        `<strong>A plan that survives.</strong> Evidence-rated, balanced, culturally rooted and measured on leading and lagging indicators with named owners. This is resilience as a sustained capability — not a programme that decays the moment cost pressure returns.`,

